    "orientation_rule": {
      "group": "site",
      "binding": "specified",
      "status": "drafted",
      "confidence": "high",
      "judgment": true,
      "rule": "Orientation follows use, sun and view as the plan itself dictates, not a fixed compass bearing for a single designed feature; a long low room is turned to end in a bright bay where the site allows it.",
      "note": "OVERRIDES a cascade fill from mediterranean-vernacular (a fixed 135-225 degree loggia-facing bearing), which is specific to that family's hot-dry-climate loggia/aia siting logic and has no basis in this style's own text -- arts-and-crafts-british sits in climate zones 4C/5A (cool, humid Britain), not the Mediterranean family's 3B/3C/2B, and its own governing_logic states plainly that the building is 'composed from the inside out around use, sun, and view' with 'no applied system,' not a fixed bearing rule. This node's own description corroborates: 'Light is handled carefully and often sparingly, so a long low room ends in a bright bay' -- a per-room, per-site judgment, not a fixed orientation figure. The Mediterranean fact reaches this node's cascade only because italian-villa-vernacular is a real, distant lineage ancestor several links up the classical-adjacent chain (see docs/inheritance.md's own worked cascade example, which shows the identical insertion point for tidewater-georgian), not because of any documented Mediterranean influence on this style. Fixed here per WP-4.2 instructions rather than on any ancestor kit."
    },
